 {"id": "TJ-REVIEW-073", "title": "[P3] resident bridge daemon leaves the socket file behind on SIGTERM (finally-unlink unreachable) — next launcher re-binds only because of the dead-socket probe; found during the review's own scratch-server stop", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_at": "2026-10-21T21:41:29Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T00:20:00Z", "created_by": null, "capability_tags": ["resident-bridge", "lifecycle"], "complexity": 3, "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "blocked_reason": null, "depends_on": [], "created_by_note": null, "reasoning": "Hands-on review 2026-10-21, scratch daemon on /tmp/tj-review-2026-10-21/interruptor.sock (pid tracked, killed with SIGTERM). serve()'s  (interruptor_resident.py:226-229) does not run on SIGTERM: default SIGTERM disposition kills the interpreter without unwinding, and no signal handler is installed. Observed twice on the review harness: after  the process is gone but  still true; the next  start succeeds only because serve()'s stale-socket probe connects, gets ECONNREFUSED, and unlinks the dead path (interruptor_resident.py:170-178). Consequences are minor but real: (a) monitoring that treats 'socket exists' as 'daemon alive' false-positives; (b) the refusal arm for a LIVE socket and the recovery arm for a dead one are distinguished only by a connect() race window; (c) two daemons racing a dead socket can unlink each other's path. Fix direction: install a SIGTERM/SIGINT handler that raises SystemExit (finally runs), or unlink via atexit, or document that the socket file is not a liveness signal. ~15 lines + a test asserting the socket is gone after SIGTERM. Evidence transcript: review report 2026-10-21, 'resident bridge: start/stop pair' section.", "description": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "05e5f360be9b505c27216d50110a94578875d05e717d9c9cf0b2d4bbca34b7fe"}, "evidence": null, "ci_result": "GREEN exact HEAD board closeout run 39698267292", "foreman_note": "Verification complete but not fully judged: implementa